module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
import sysl.text.str_builder

// The walk: every statement, checked against what `fits.sysl` knows.
//
// **`fits.sysl` holds the rule this pass is built on** and is worth reading first -- the check may
// only report what the machine would also refuse, and every relation there mirrors a run-time test
// exactly. What is NOT checked yet is listed at the foot of this file.
//
// **What the builtins and the imports are known to be is in `signatures.sysl`**, cut out when this
// file reached a thousand lines: that one answers what a name nothing declared is, this one walks.
//
// **A CALL IS IN `calls.sysl`**, cut out when it passed a thousand again -- a signature, what a call
// answers, what its arguments are checked against, and the method spelling, which is the same call
// with the receiver in front.
//
// **AND AN EXPRESSION IS IN `check_expr.sysl`**, cut out at the third thousand, on the heading this
// file already had. What is left here is where the pass GOES -- a file, a block, a statement, a
// binding -- together with what a NAME is known to hold, which is a statement's question rather than
// an expression's: `var_type` reads the assignments in the block that declared the name, and
// `elementless` gives up an element type a literal supplied because the name outlives the line.
// Four names cross to the expressions and three come back, counted with the comments stripped.

// -- the walk -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Check one file's statements. Reported through the compiler's own channel, so a complaint from here
// stops the program exactly as a parse error does.
check_program(prog: Program, internal: bool)
    var env = type_env()

    // **THE BUILTINS ARE ASKED OF THE INSTALLER RATHER THAN LISTED AGAIN.** An undefined-name check
    // needs to know every name a program starts with, and `install_builtin_types` below carries only
    // the ones whose *type* is worth stating -- `print`, `assert`, `min` and five others deliberately
    // say nothing and would have looked undefined. A second list of 140 names would drift the first
    // time one was added; this builds a real scope with the real installer and asks it.
    //
    // **`install_core` and not `install_builtins`: a name a module has taken is NOT one a program
    // starts with.** `date(2026, 1, 1)` with no `import ... from slate:time` above it is a fault the
    // machine raises, so this pass has to see it too -- and asking the wrong installer would have
    // made the migration silent exactly where it needs to be loud.
    //
    // **ONE OF SLATE'S OWN MODULES IS THE EXCEPTION, and it is `run_modules`' exception in the same
    // words.** A slate-source module is compiled against every builtin, because the seam it is
    // written against -- `httpParser` and the rest -- is deliberately not part of the language. Ask
    // the wrong installer here and the module cannot see what it is made of.
    builtin_scope = new_env(null)

    if internal then install_builtins(builtin_scope) else install_core(builtin_scope)

    // **A SECOND COPY OF THE BUILTIN TYPES, WHICH A PROGRAM CANNOT SHADOW.** An array's `.filter` is
    // the builtin whatever the program has named `filter`, so looking the method's signature up in
    // the walking scope would check `xs.filter(p)` against a function the program wrote. There is no
    // array prototype and a program cannot add to the table, which is what makes this the right
    // answer rather than a convenience.
    builtin_types = type_env()

    enter_scope(&builtin_types)
    install_builtin_types(&builtin_types)
    enter_scope(&env)
    install_builtin_types(&env)
    check_stmts(prog.stmts, &env)
    leave_scope(&env)
end check_program

// The scope every program starts in, built once per check. **Module storage because the walk reaches
// it from everywhere**, and a root nothing else refers to -- it is thrown away when the next program
// is checked.
var builtin_scope: *EnvObj = null

// The builtins' TYPES, kept apart from the walking scope so that a name the program took cannot be
// mistaken for the builtin a method call means.
var builtin_types: TypeEnv = type_env()

// Whether a program could find this name at all: something it wrote, or something it was given.
//
// Not `private`: reached from `check_expr.sysl`.
defined(env: *TypeEnv, name: string) -> bool
    if has_name(env, name) then return true
    if builtin_scope == null then return true

    knows(builtin_scope, name)

// The shape of the object a `catch` binds. **`message`, `line` and `file`, always** -- `throw` turns
// whatever a program raised into the same three fields every other fault already leaves.
//
// Not `private`: reached from `check_expr.sysl`.
fault_shape() -> Type
    var keys: Buf[string] = buf()
    var types: Buf[Type] = buf()
    var optional: Buf[bool] = buf()

    keys.push("message")
    types.push(type_of(StringType))
    optional.push(false)
    keys.push("line")
    types.push(type_of(IntegerType))
    optional.push(false)
    keys.push("file")
    types.push(type_of(StringType))
    optional.push(false)

    type_of(ObjectWith(keys, types, optional))
end fault_shape

// Every name a pattern introduces, as `Any`.
//
// **This is shadowing rather than typing.** A pattern's bindings are not given types yet -- what
// matters here is that a name a pattern took is no longer whatever an outer scope said it was, and
// most of all is no longer a builtin's signature. Without it, `for len in xs` would leave `len`
// looking like the builtin and the next call to it would be counted against the wrong thing.
//
// Not `private`: reached from `check_expr.sysl`.
note_bindings(pat: Pattern, e: *TypeEnv)
    *pat.kind match
        // **A bare name says nothing about what it caught**, which is the whole of its job: it
        // matches anything, so anything is what is known about it.
        Bind(name) -> note_type(e, name, type_of(Any))

        // **`n @ int` is a test AND a name, so the name has the test's type.** This is the one place
        // a pattern says something a walk can use, and it is the spelling slate already had for
        // testing and naming at once -- so `n @ int -> n.push(1)` is refused before it runs, by the
        // method-name check that is this pass's best feature.
        //
        // **Resolved first, so a declared type or a class name works here too.** `q @ Square` reads
        // through `resolve_pattern` exactly as an annotation does; without that the name would be a
        // binding and the answer `Any`.
        At(name, inner) ->
            note_type(e, name, type_from_pattern(resolve_pattern(inner, 0)))
            note_bindings(inner, e)
        ArrayPat(items, _, rest, _) ->
            for i in 0..<items.len()
                note_bindings(items.at(i), e)

            // **What `...rest` catches is an ARRAY, whatever is in it.** The pattern says so on its
            // own -- no subject needed -- so this is the one binding in a pattern that has a type
            // without anybody writing one. `val [a, ...rest] = xs` then `rest.upper()` is refused.
            rest match
                Some(name) -> note_type(e, name, type_of(ArrayOf(type_of(Any))))
                None -> ()
        ObjectPat(_, values, _, _) ->
            for i in 0..<values.len()
                note_bindings(values.at(i), e)
        OrPat(alts) ->
            for i in 0..<alts.len()
                note_bindings(alts.at(i), e)
        AndPat(parts) ->
            for i in 0..<parts.len()
                note_bindings(parts.at(i), e)

        // A variant's fields are patterns like any others, and missing this made every name a
        // `Circle(r)` bound look undefined -- by the check that exists to catch a name nothing bound.
        VariantPat(_, items, _, _) ->
            for i in 0..<items.len()
                note_bindings(items.at(i), e)
        MadeFrom(_, inner) -> note_bindings(inner, e)
        _ -> ()
end note_bindings

// **Every definition in a block is noted before any statement is walked**, so a function may call
// one written below it and one written above may call it back. Whether the *machine* has run the
// definition by then is a different question and a different fault.
//
// Not `private`: reached from `check_expr.sysl`.
check_stmts(stmts: Buf[Stmt], env: *TypeEnv)
    refuse_redefinitions(stmts)

    for i in 0..<stmts.len()
        *stmts.at(i).kind match
            Def(name, params, defaults, body, is_async) ->
                note_type(env, name, signature_of(params, floor_of(params, defaults.len()), body_at(body), is_async))

            // **EVERY name this block binds is noted before any body is walked, not only the
            // definitions.** A closure may name something declared later in the block it sits in --
            //
            //     val probe = { equals: o -> clear(xs) }
            //     val xs = [probe]
            //
            // -- which runs, because the body runs after `xs` exists. Checking that body against the
            // scope as it stood where the closure was *written* would refuse it, and refusing a
            // program that runs is the one thing this pass may not do. The type is `Any` here and the
            // real one is noted in the walk below, which shadows this by being nearer.
            Val(name, _) -> note_type(env, name, type_of(Any))
            Var(name, _) -> note_binding(env, name, type_of(Any), false)
            Unpack(pat, _, _) -> note_bindings(pat, env)

            // A group holds nothing but the `val`s one `data` line wrote, so this is the `Val` arm
            // above applied to each of them -- and it has to be here, or a function written above a
            // data declaration could not name one of its variants.
            Group(stmts) ->
                for j in 0..<stmts.len()
                    *stmts.at(j).kind match
                        Val(name, _) -> note_type(env, name, type_of(Any))

                        // **An annotated binding is a group as well now**, the parser writing the
                        // binding and the check it turns into as one statement -- so a `var` has to
                        // be noted here for the reason a `val` is.
                        Var(name, _) -> note_binding(env, name, type_of(Any), false)
                        _ -> ()
            ImportNames(path, _, origin, names, locals) ->
                for j in 0..<locals.len()
                    note_type(env, locals.at(j), import_signature(path, origin, names.at(j)))
            ImportAll(_, _, _, alias) -> note_type(env, alias, type_of(Any))

            // An asset is a string and the pass may say so, the loader having read it already.
            ImportAsset(_, _, name) -> note_type(env, name, type_of(StringType))

            // **A type binds a value under its own name**, so a function written above the
            // declaration may name it -- which is the whole reason this is in the pre-pass rather
            // than only in the walk below.
            TypeDecl(name, _) -> note_type(env, name, shape_type())
            _ -> ()

    for i in 0..<stmts.len()
        check_stmt(stmts.at(i), stmts, env)
        guard_narrows(stmts.at(i), env)
end check_stmts

// **A FUNCTION DECLARATION MAY NOT SHARE A NAME WITH ANOTHER DECLARATION IN THE SAME BLOCK.**
//
// Definitions are hoisted -- bound before the block runs, as JavaScript binds them -- and everything
// else in a block is bound where it stands. So two declarations of one name, where either of them is
// a definition, do not mean what they look like they mean, and the two back ends did not even agree
// about what they did mean:
//
//     f() = 1
//     print(f())
//     f() = 2
//     print(f())
//
// printed `2 2` under the interpreter, whose `DefineFn` overwrites the binding silently as it
// hoists, and `1 2` under the JavaScript back end, whose declarations are rebound in order. Neither
// is wrong given the other's rule and no rule makes both right, which is what makes this a mistake
// to refuse rather than a behaviour to pick.
//
// **BOTH SITES ARE COMPLAINED ABOUT**, so the report names both lines with a caret under each -- the
// second one saying what is wrong and the first saying where the name already was. A span here is a
// pair of offsets and knows no line number; the report is what turns one into a line, so two
// complaints is how this pass names two lines.
//
// **What is refused is the LATER declaration being a definition**, `val f = 1` above `f() = 2`
// included -- the definition is bound first whatever the text says, so neither of those reads the
// way it is written.
//
// **THE OTHER ORDER IS AN IDIOM AND STAYS.** A definition with a `val` of the same name BELOW it is
// how a generator is named and then run --
//
//     g()
//         yield 1
//
//     val g = g()
//
// -- and it means exactly what it looks like: the function is hoisted, the `val` runs where it
// stands, and both back ends already agree about it. `tests_lang.sysl` has one and it found this
// rule written too wide.
//
// **A block is one run of statements**, so a definition inside a nested function or a nested block is
// its own -- shadowing an outer name is unaffected, and that is the ordinary reason to reuse one.
private refuse_redefinitions(stmts: Buf[Stmt])
    var names: Buf[string] = buf()
    var spans: Buf[Span] = buf()
    var defs: Buf[bool] = buf()

    for i in 0..<stmts.len()
        declared_by(stmts.at(i), &names, &spans, &defs)

    for i in 0..<names.len()
        for j in (i + 1)..<names.len()
            if names.at(i) == names.at(j) && defs.at(j)
                complain_here(spans.at(j), s"`${names.at(j)}` is declared twice in this block, and a definition may not take a name another declaration in it already has -- a definition is bound before the block runs, so the two do not run in the order they are written")
                complain_here(spans.at(i), s"`${names.at(i)}` is declared here, and the definition below takes the same name")

                return
end refuse_redefinitions

// The names one statement declares in the block it stands in, and whether each is a DEFINITION --
// which is the half that is hoisted.
private declared_by(s: Stmt, names: *Buf[string], spans: *Buf[Span], defs: *Buf[bool])
    *s.kind match
        Def(name, _, _, _, _) ->
            names.push(name)
            spans.push(s.span)
            defs.push(true)
        Val(name, _) ->
            names.push(name)
            spans.push(s.span)
            defs.push(false)
        Var(name, _) ->
            names.push(name)
            spans.push(s.span)
            defs.push(false)

        // **A group binds where it stands rather than in a scope of its own**, which is what a
        // `data` declaration and an annotated binding are made of -- so its statements are this
        // block's declarations and are walked as such.
        Group(inner) ->
            for i in 0..<inner.len()
                declared_by(inner.at(i), names, spans, defs)
        _ -> ()
end declared_by

// **A GUARD CLAUSE NARROWS WHAT FOLLOWS IT**, which is the shape nearly every function in slate's
// own modules is written in:
//
//     val rel = safePath(asked)
//
//     if rel == null then return { status: 403, body: "Forbidden" }
//
//     joinPath(root, rel)
//
// Narrowing inside an `if`'s two branches is not enough for that, because the interesting half is
// what comes *after* the statement. It is sound for the reason the whole pass is: where the branch
// always leaves, reaching the line below it means the condition was false, so the rest of the block
// is exactly the else branch written out flat.
//
// **A guard with an `else` is not a guard** -- both halves rejoin below it and nothing more is known
// there than was known above.
private guard_narrows(s: Stmt, env: *TypeEnv)
    *s.kind match
        Eval(x) ->
            *x.kind match
                IfExpr(cond, then_branch, else_branch) ->
                    else_branch match
                        Some(_) -> ()
                        None ->
                            if always_leaves(then_branch) then narrow_by(cond, false, env)
                _ -> ()
        _ -> ()
end guard_narrows

// Whether control that reaches the end of this branch has left the block the branch is in.
//
// **The LAST statement is the whole of the question.** A branch ending in a `return`, a `throw`, a
// `break` or a `continue` cannot fall out of its bottom, and anything that jumped out earlier has
// left as well. A branch that leaves only sometimes says nothing here, which is the safe answer.
private always_leaves(x: Expr) -> bool
    *x.kind match
        Block(stmts) ->
            if stmts.len() == 0 then return false

            *stmts.at(stmts.len() - 1).kind match
                Return(_) -> true
                Throw(_) -> true
                Break(_, _) -> true
                Continue(_) -> true
                _ -> false
        _ -> false
end always_leaves

// The type of a definition, read back off the checks its annotations were desugared into.
//
// **The annotations are not on the `Def` and must not be put there.** The parser turns `f(x: string)`
// into a `CheckType` statement at the top of the body and `-> string` into an `Ensures` around it,
// which is what keeps every node downstream of a definition free of them. So this reads them back,
// and a parameter with no `CheckType` is `Any` -- which is what an unannotated parameter means.
private check_stmt(s: Stmt, block: Buf[Stmt], env: *TypeEnv)
    *s.kind match
        Val(name, init) ->
            check_expr(init, env)

            val t = type_of_expr(init, env)

            // **A SHAPE IS KEPT WHERE NOTHING CAN MAKE IT STALE.** `note_type` drops one, because an
            // object is mutable and a field may be added to it anywhere -- by the next line, by a
            // function it was passed to, through a second name for the same object. None of those
            // can have happened to a name that is never mentioned except to read a field off it,
            // which is what `shape_survives` asks the block.
            if is_shape(t) && shape_survives(uses_in(name, block))
                note_shaped(env, name, t)
            else
                note_type(env, name, elementless(t, init))
        Var(name, init) ->
            check_expr(init, env)
            note_binding(env, name, elementless(var_type(name, init, block, env), init), false)
        Group(stmts) ->
            for i in 0..<stmts.len()
                check_stmt(stmts.at(i), stmts, env)

                // **An annotated binding is a `Group` of exactly two: the binding, then the check
                // it turns into.** The pairing is the parser's, and reading it back here is what
                // lets the initialiser be compared against the type the reader declared -- the same
                // comparison the machine makes one instruction later, so the two cannot disagree.
                if i > 0
                    *stmts.at(i).kind match
                        CheckType(name, _, pat) -> check_declared_init(stmts.at(i - 1), name, pat, env)
                        _ -> ()
        Unpack(pat, init, _) ->
            check_expr(init, env)
            note_bindings(pat, env)
        Assign(op, target, value) ->
            check_expr(target, env)
            check_expr(value, env)

            // **AN ASSIGNMENT IS CHECKED AGAINST WHAT THE NAME WAS DECLARED, WHICH IS WHAT AN
            // ANNOTATED `var` MEANS.** TypeScript's `let`: the declared type is what the name holds
            // for its whole life, so a value of another kind is refused where it is written rather
            // than surfacing wherever it was first used.
            //
            // **An UNANNOTATED `var` cannot be caught out by this**, its recorded type being the
            // union of every right-hand side ever assigned to it -- this one included -- so the
            // comparison is one that cannot fail. What is left is exactly the case the reader asked
            // to be held to.
            //
            // **A compound assignment is left alone.** `n += 1` hands over the right OPERAND rather
            // than the value stored, and what the operator answers is `binary_type`'s question and
            // is checked there.
            if op == Tok.Assign
                *target.kind match
                    Name(name) ->
                        val want = known_type(env, name)
                        val got = type_of_expr(value, env)

                        if !fits(got, want)
                            complain_here(value.span, s"`${name}` was declared ${show_type(want)}, and this is ${show_type(got)}")
                    _ -> ()
        MultiAssign(_, targets, values) ->
            for i in 0..<targets.len()
                check_expr(targets.at(i), env)

            for i in 0..<values.len()
                check_expr(values.at(i), env)
        Def(_, params, defaults, body, _) -> check_body(params, defaults, body_at(body), env, buf())

        // **An imported name is `Any` and, more to the point, is no longer a builtin's signature.**
        // A file that imports its own `map` would otherwise be checked against slate's -- and one of
        // slate's own modules is the exception, its exports being the builtins under another name.
        ImportNames(path, _, origin, names, locals) ->
            for i in 0..<locals.len()
                note_type(env, locals.at(i), import_signature(path, origin, names.at(i)))
        ImportAll(_, _, _, alias) -> note_type(env, alias, type_of(Any))
        ImportAsset(_, _, name) -> note_type(env, name, type_of(StringType))
        Eval(x) -> check_expr(x, env)
        Return(value) -> value match
            Some(x) -> check_expr(x, env)
            None -> ()
        Throw(x) -> check_expr(x, env)
        TypeDecl(name, _) -> note_type(env, name, shape_type())

        // **What an annotation says, wherever one was written.** A parameter's is already in scope by
        // the time this is walked -- `check_body` reads it off this very statement before the body is
        // entered -- so re-noting it says the same thing twice. A BINDING's is not: the group the
        // parser made binds the name first at whatever its initialiser gave, and this is what makes
        // it the type the reader declared instead.
        CheckType(name, _, pat) -> renote_declared(env, name, type_from_pattern(resolve_pattern(pat, 0)))
        _ -> ()
end check_stmt

// An annotated binding's initialiser, against the type the annotation declared.
//
// **The complaint lands on the VALUE and not on the annotation**, because the annotation is the thing
// the reader meant and the value is the thing that is wrong. It is the same choice a call's complaint
// makes, and it reads the same way: what was wanted, then what arrived.
private check_declared_init(bound: Stmt, name: string, pat: Pattern, env: *TypeEnv)
    val init = *bound.kind match
        Val(who, x) -> if who == name then Some(x) else None
        Var(who, x) -> if who == name then Some(x) else None
        _ -> None

    init match
        Some(x) ->
            val want = type_from_pattern(resolve_pattern(pat, 0))
            val got = type_of_expr(x, env)

            // **The unnamed field is asked about FIRST, and its refusal stands instead of this
            // one.** A misspelled field is missing and spare at once, so the two would name the
            // same mistake twice -- and the sentence that quotes the two shapes side by side is the
            // less useful half, having nothing to say about which spelling was meant.
            if !refuse_excess_fields(x, want) && !fits(got, want)
                complain_here(x.span, s"`${name}` was declared ${show_type(want)}, and this is ${show_type(got)}")
        None -> ()
end check_declared_init

// A definition's body, with its parameters in scope at the types its annotations gave them.
//
// **A default is code the program wrote and is walked like any other**, so a name it gets wrong is
// caught where it is written rather than at whichever call first leaves that argument out. Every
// parameter is in scope for it, which is wider than the run-time truth -- a default sees only the
// ones to its left -- and wider is the safe direction: this pass may not refuse a program that runs.
//
// **`expect` is EMPTY for a definition and carries something only for a LAMBDA WRITTEN AT A CALL**,
// where the callee's signature says what its callback is handed. A definition is called from
// anywhere and there is no such caller to ask.

private is_shape(t: Type) -> bool = *t.kind match
    ObjectWith(keys, _, _) ->
        for i in 0..<keys.len()
            if keys.at(i) == "proto" || keys.at(i) == DataTag || keys.at(i) == ClassTag || keys.at(i) == ShapeTag then return false

        true
    _ -> false

// A NAME's type, with an element type a LITERAL gave it dropped again.
//
// **An array is mutable and a name outlives the line that built it**, so `val xs = [1]` followed by
// `xs[0] = "a"` holds an array of strings -- and a recorded `array of integer` would then refuse a
// program the machine runs, which is the one mistake this pass may not make. It is the rule an
// object's shape already follows, applied to the one other type this pass reads out of a literal.
//
// **What is NOT dropped is what an annotation says**, here or on a parameter: that is a promise the
// machine checks where the value arrives, and a program that breaks it later breaks it against the
// same annotation. Only what was INFERRED from a literal standing right here is given up.
private elementless(t: Type, init: Expr) -> Type
    *init.kind match
        ArrayLit(_) -> ()
        ArrayRepeat(_, _) -> ()
        _ -> return t

    *t.kind match
        ArrayOf(_) -> type_of(ArrayOf(type_of(Any)))
        _ -> t
end elementless

// WHAT A `var` MAY EVER HOLD, which is the union of its initialiser and every right-hand side ever
// assigned to it.
//
// **The pass can follow assignments after all, and does not have to follow them in ORDER.** What
// stopped a `var` being typed was that a later assignment may put anything there -- so this reads
// the later assignments. Every one of them is written in the block that declared the name, a local
// being reachable from nowhere else, so the union is complete however the program branches and
// whatever order the lines run in. Nothing here is narrowing: a `var` is still `settled: false` and
// `may_narrow` still refuses it, because a *branch* may make a recorded type stale where the union
// of everything cannot.
//
// **Anything less than certain gives up at once**, which is nearly all of the code below: a union of
// two kinds is not something to argue from, and a type this pass would not argue from is `Any`.
private var_type(name: string, init: Expr, block: Buf[Stmt], env: *TypeEnv) -> Type
    val u = uses_in(name, block)

    // A multiple assignment says which value lands on which target by counting, and this pass does
    // not count.
    if u.murky then return type_of(Any)

    val t = type_of_expr(init, env)

    if !certain(t) then return type_of(Any)

    for i in 0..<u.ops.len()
        val given = type_of_expr(u.values.at(i), env)

        // **A compound assignment is the operator it carries**, `+=` holding `Plus` exactly as
        // `compile_assign` reads it -- so what lands in the name is what that operator answers, and
        // `binary_type` is the same table the machine works to. Using the initialiser as the left
        // operand is exact rather than an approximation: the loop gives up unless every assignment
        // leaves the same type, so if it finishes the name held that type at every step.
        val landed = if u.ops.at(i) == Assign then given else binary_type(u.ops.at(i), t, given)

        if !same_type(landed, t) then return type_of(Any)

    // `x++` is `x + 1`, and on anything but a number it is not this type any more.
    if u.bumps > 0 && !same_type(binary_type(Plus, t, type_of(IntegerType)), t)
        return type_of(Any)

    t
end var_type

// -- what this pass does NOT do yet ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Written down because the absences are decisions rather than oversights, and because each one is
// the next piece of work rather than a thing to be surprised by.
//
// - **A name bound by a PATTERN has no type.** A `match` arm, a `catch`, a destructuring binding and
//   a `for` head all introduce names the walk leaves as `Any`. The patterns already carry the
//   information -- `type_from_pattern` would answer for most of them -- and threading it in is the
//   obvious next step.
// - ~~**A `var` is always `Any`**~~ and ~~**a field read is `Any`**~~ -- both were the same fact
//   about mutation, and both are answered by `flow.sysl` reading the rest of the block rather than
//   by looking harder at the declaration. A `var` is the union of its initialiser and every
//   right-hand side ever assigned to it, which does not depend on the order the lines run in; a
//   `val`'s shape is kept where its name is never mentioned except to read a field off it, so
//   nothing else can hold the object and no field of it is ever written. What is still `Any`:
//   **a `var` the program reassigns to another kind** (a union of two kinds is not something to
//   argue from), **an annotated PARAMETER's shape** (the object came from the caller, who may hold
//   another name for it), and **a field of an object reached through anything but a bare name**.
// - **A function's RESULT is not checked against its body.** `-> integer` is enforced by
//   `CheckResult` at run time and believed here. A LAMBDA's result is now inferred FROM its body,
//   which is a different question -- it has no way to say one, so there is nothing to believe.
// - ~~**A LAMBDA'S PARAMETERS ARE `Any`**~~ -- a lambda written where a signature says what its
//   callback is handed gets those types instead, so `map(ns, n -> n * 2)` is an `array of integer`
//   and a mistake inside the body is caught. What is still `Any` is a lambda **anywhere else**: one
//   bound to a name, one stored in an object, one handed to a function the program wrote. Carrying
//   the expectation to those is a different piece of work -- the callee is not a signature this pass
//   holds, so there would be nothing to read it off.
// - **A CALLBACK'S RESULT IS NOT CHECKED, and exactly one builtin would notice.** `sorted(xs, (a,
//   b) -> a.name)` is refused by the machine in as many words -- a comparator answers whether the
//   first comes before the second -- and this pass says nothing, every callback signature answering
//   `Any`. **`sort` and `sorted` are the whole list**: `filter`, `find`, `every`, `some`, `map`,
//   `flatMap` and `forEach` all read what they are handed with `truthy()` or store it, so any value
//   at all is a legal answer to them. It would also need `fits` to compare a function's RESULT,
//   which it deliberately does not do today.
// - **A NAMED ARGUMENT is not placed.** `FunctionType` carries parameter types and not parameter
//   names, so `f(b = 1)` cannot be matched to a slot here and the per-argument type check steps
//   aside for the whole call. The count is still checked, that being the same between the two ends
//   whichever way the arguments were written. **Carrying the names in `FunctionType` is the way
//   in**, and it would pay for itself twice: the machine's own message names the parameter, and
//   this pass could say the same thing before the program runs.
// - **Narrowing reaches an `if` and a `match` arm and nothing else.** A `while`, a guard and the
//   right-hand side of `||` all leave the subject as it was.
// - **A temporal operator is not checked at all.** What `+`, `-` and `*` mean over the eight
//   temporal kinds is a table in `time.sysl`, and restating it here would be a second copy kept in
//   step by hand -- so `check_binary` steps aside for them. Reading that table rather than copying
//   it is the way in, if it is ever worth doing.
//
// **A METHOD NAME IS CHECKED NOW, AND IT IS THE MOST USEFUL THING THIS PASS DOES.** `"abc".push(1)`
// and `date(2026, 1, 1).hour()` are refused before they run, which is the commonest mistake there is
// in a language with per-kind method tables. It is sound because it asks the machine's own question:
// `methods_of` in `method.sysl` is the table `builtin_method` dispatches through, so a name added to
// a kind is added in one place and the two can never disagree. An object is never asked about -- its
// names are the program's -- and nor is a receiver whose type is not certain.
